<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Visegrád Group</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Visegrád Four</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> Group is a regional alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czechia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, founded at Visegrád in Hungary in February 1991 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, Hungary and Poland. Its name recalls the meeting of the kings of Hungary, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and Poland at Visegrád in 1335. It helped coordinate its members' path into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and continues to promote cooperation in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/visegrad-group.jpg" alt="Visegrád Group" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CrazyPhunk · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visegrad_group_countries.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name refers to the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> of 1335, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-hungary">Charles I of Hungary</a>, Casimir III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and John I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> agreed to create new commercial routes bypassing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, a staple port that required goods to be offered for sale in the city. The congress also confirmed Czech sovereignty over the Duchy of Silesia. A second congress in 1339 decided that if Casimir died without a son, as happened in 1370, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a>, son of Charles I, would become King of Poland.</p><p>From the 16th century large parts of the later members' territories came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> dissolved in 1918. After 1945 the three countries became Soviet satellite states: the Polish People's Republic, the Hungarian People's Republic and the Czechoslovak Socialist Republic. Communism fell in central and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1989.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The group was founded on 15 February 1991 at a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visegrad">Visegrád</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaclav-havel">Václav Havel</a>, president of the Czech and Slovak Federative Republic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>, president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozsef-antall">József Antall</a>, prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Its aim was to move away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> and carry out the reforms needed for full membership of Euro-Atlantic institutions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> dissolved in 1993, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> became separate members, raising the number from three to four. All four joined the European Union on 1 May 2004, achieving the group's main goal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schengen-area">Schengen Area</a> in 2007.</p><p>After 2004 the group concentrated on cultural cooperation and on expert-level work on topics such as infrastructure. In 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s defence minister announced a Visegrád EU Battlegroup, which stood on standby in 2016, and a patent institute was created by an agreement signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a> on 26 February 2015.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The International Visegrád Fund is the group's only institutionalised form of cooperation. It supports regional non-governmental initiatives to strengthen ties among people and institutions in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> has been one of its largest recipients. The group has also been a model for others: the Craiova Group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, established in 2015, was inspired by it. Its members share a close trading relationship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, particularly in car manufacturing, and the group's networking among public servants and civil society actors has outlasted changes of government.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Visegrád Group” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Since 1989 Hungary's chief foreign aim has been integration into Western institutions: it joined NATO in 1999 and the European Union in 2004, and belongs to the OECD, the WTO, the <b>Visegrád Group</b> and the Central European Defence Cooperation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…by marriage and personal union with Naples, Poland, Bohemia and the Holy Roman Empire, and the meeting of the kings of Hungary, Bohemia and Poland at Visegrád in 1335 is remembered as an early example of central European cooperation, the namesake of the <b>Visegrád Group</b> founded in 1991.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
